 {"question" "q-130285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd8e91097d8e18f339ddd8dac5a808f98b3e723f27a8678f536270996a6199c5"} {"question" "q-130285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "50aed26ef029541e3c6bca4dd3bfce4485a93dd5f658a4efb76b88150a4cd32d"} {"question" "q-130285" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd8e91097d8e18f339ddd8dac5a808f98b3e723f27a8678f536270996a6199c5"} {"question" "q-130285" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}"] ["math" "{R}_{3}:=(-2){R}_{3}"] ["math" "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd8e91097d8e18f339ddd8dac5a808f98b3e723f27a8678f536270996a6199c5"} {"question" "q-130285" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd8e91097d8e18f339ddd8dac5a808f98b3e723f27a8678f536270996a6199c5"} {"question" "q-130283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n$X=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 10 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{13}=0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd8e91097d8e18f339ddd8dac5a808f98b3e723f27a8678f536270996a6199c5"} {"question" "q-130283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 10 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd8e91097d8e18f339ddd8dac5a808f98b3e723f27a8678f536270996a6199c5"} {"question" "q-130283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "50aed26ef029541e3c6bca4dd3bfce4485a93dd5f658a4efb76b88150a4cd32d"} {"question" "q-130283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd8e91097d8e18f339ddd8dac5a808f98b3e723f27a8678f536270996a6199c5"} {"question" "q-130283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{1}:=(-2){R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}"] ["math" "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd8e91097d8e18f339ddd8dac5a808f98b3e723f27a8678f536270996a6199c5"} {"question" "q-130283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd8e91097d8e18f339ddd8dac5a808f98b3e723f27a8678f536270996a6199c5"} {"question" "e-4693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/assets/627d12115d61ddfdedf9db08330269de2137a23abceada977d039e90e5b88675.png)\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2 := R_2 +(- 4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/example-4693.json" "file_sha256" "98fbf7877a00c1c2c257e5ac45652f781d05804b993958f0a9ec61cf579920c5"} {"question" "e-4693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1$ means that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4+(-4)(1) & 2+(-4)(2) & -1+(-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/example-4693.json" "file_sha256" "98fbf7877a00c1c2c257e5ac45652f781d05804b993958f0a9ec61cf579920c5"} {"question" "e-4694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/assets/82729e1d34174d58f01547b58aa63db28ec68a4e1964ef5cd127a2167ed4a722.png) \n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ on $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/example-4694.json" "file_sha256" "f7ebea4e3c4105e2072c790e2badb2b4d3921ca206fe4258e680eb833abc6673"} {"question" "e-4694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/example-4694.json" "file_sha256" "f7ebea4e3c4105e2072c790e2badb2b4d3921ca206fe4258e680eb833abc6673"} {"question" "e-4695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/assets/56fd7e343edf6c26f36718a30f899af6c8eaf0cad5f6b07c90ffa93c19df6346.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/example-4695.json" "file_sha256" "8c7618d7902f2730d017783198b179537c9fd341b9b2e0dea97ec19faa335535"} {"question" "e-4695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nNow, applying the operation $R_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4+1\\cdot (-4) & 1+1\\cdot (-2) & 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation \n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/example-4695.json" "file_sha256" "8c7618d7902f2730d017783198b179537c9fd341b9b2e0dea97ec19faa335535"} {"question" "e-4696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/assets/59ee8e91d058e347238320a2bd5496630f33534564f67f901d2ddf89000f0108.png) which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/example-4696.json" "file_sha256" "80635f01092da710fc696acbe48d15d97df93c7a3be97edba654f6c0db59adb6"} {"question" "e-4696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0.$\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0,$ because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0.$\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1.$ If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22},$ we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1.\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1+(-5)\\cdot 3 & 5+(-5)\\cdot 1 & 6+(-5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1,\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 -5 R_1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071448/example-4696.json" "file_sha256" "80635f01092da710fc696acbe48d15d97df93c7a3be97edba654f6c0db59adb6"}] "usage" {"q-130103" [] "e-4695" [] "q-130087" [] "q-130283" [] "q-130072" [] "e-4696" [] "q-10248" [] "e-4694" [] "e-4693" []} "basis" 2560 "retractions" [[17592186059504 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2 := R_2 +(- 4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A?$"] [17592186059505 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ on $A?$"] [17592186059506 :question/problem "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186059507 :question/problem "Given that\n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0?$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186056037 17592186056038 17592186056039 17592186056040 17592186056041 17592186303761 17592186303762 17592186303763 17592186303764 17592186303765 17592186303768 17592186303769 17592186303770 17592186303771 17592186303772 17592186303789 17592186303790 17592186303791 17592186303792 17592186303793 17592186303796 17592186303797 17592186303798 17592186303799 17592186303800] "immutable_fields" [[17592186056036 :answer-field/correct] [17592186056036 :answer-field/id] [17592186056036 :answer-field/key] [17592186056036 :answer-field/type] [17592186303760 :answer-field/correct] [17592186303760 :answer-field/id] [17592186303760 :answer-field/key] [17592186303760 :answer-field/type] [17592186303767 :answer-field/correct] [17592186303767 :answer-field/id] [17592186303767 :answer-field/key] [17592186303767 :answer-field/type] [17592186303788 :answer-field/correct] [17592186303788 :answer-field/id] [17592186303788 :answer-field/key] [17592186303788 :answer-field/type] [17592186303795 :answer-field/correct] [17592186303795 :answer-field/id] [17592186303795 :answer-field/key] [17592186303795 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
